# Le fichier magique UR est lu par le controleur Linux du robot : un CRLF
# sur la ligne shebang casse l interpreteur. Il reste en LF sur tout poste,
# quelle que soit la configuration core.autocrlf de la machine.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows batch scripts run under cmd.exe, which is not forgiving of a bare
# LF; force CRLF regardless of the writing workstation's core.autocrlf.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# C sources, Python modules, Markdown and JSON are plain LF text. Nothing
# here is read by a controller that cares about the newline byte, so there
# is no reason to let a workstation's line-ending setting turn an unrelated
# edit into a whole-file diff.
*.c text eol=lf
*.h text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.xacro text eol=lf

# Repo bookkeeping files carry no extension of their own.
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf

# Binary mesh / image / archive formats: never let git compute a text diff
# or rewrite a byte of these on checkout.
*.stl binary
*.STL binary
*.dae binary
*.png binary
*.pptx binary
*.exe binary

# Generated URScript. Settled by F10 on 2026-08-16, the fix that owned this
# question. `_write_export` emits LF, the UR controller reads the file on
# Linux, and the reproducibility tests compare a generated string against the
# tracked file byte for byte. Without this rule `core.autocrlf=true` hands a
# Windows clone a CRLF copy, and those tests fail on a fresh checkout for a
# reason that has nothing to do with the code under test.
*.script text eol=lf

# etalement.urp keeps no rule yet, on purpose. It is hand-tuned by the
# operator for robot trials, so renormalising it rewrites an artifact that
# nobody generated; that is an operator decision, not a side effect of F10.
